    "_warning": "This file is GENERATED and is a control-set member (every refusal in this harness is final). Do not hand-edit for permanent change: edit settings.template.json in the Ratchet source and re-run install.sh, or the file will be overwritten on upgrade. The agent cannot write it at all (deny below + guard.sh rule control-layer)."
  },
  "_ratchet_permission_doctrine": "READ THIS BEFORE 'FIXING' ANYTHING IN allow[]. The allow list is not a list of things we consider safe. It is the AVAILABILITY SURFACE for unattended operation. Under defaultMode=acceptEdits an unlisted Bash command raises an interactive PERMISSION PROMPT, and a prompt in an unattended run is an indefinite stall indistinguishable, from the outside, from an agent that is working. So several commands are deliberately ALLOWED here and then REFUSED by .claude/hooks/guard.sh. That is not a misconfiguration: it converts a silent stall into a loud, machine-readable refusal that names the rule and says what to do instead. Concretely: 'git config:*', 'git remote:*', inline interpreters, deletions outside .pipeline/, compound git/gh forms, gh verbs outside the standing read surface, and writes under .claude/** are ALL allowed here and ALL refused by guard.sh. Remove them from allow[] and the guard never runs, because the permission layer stalls first - you would have traded a refusal the agent can act on for a prompt nobody is there to answer.",
  "_ratchet_deny_partition": "EVERY REFUSAL IN THIS HARNESS IS FINAL. The escalation channel - an HMAC-signed, single-use, human-minted approval that could lift one specific refusal - was removed 2026-08-24. All 15 rules it could lift were audited and none needed a human: each was either something the agent can do correctly itself (name the push target, split the compound command, amend the manifest), something a fixed policy should decide (repo-local git identity is fine, changing the remote is not), or something that should never have been liftable. A channel that pauses an autonomous run to ask permission for a decision no human was adding judgment to is a stall, not a safety feature. Two consequences. (1) A settings deny CANNOT be lifted at runtime - deny beats ask and allow, no hook overrides it, and the agent cannot edit this file - and that is now the same statement as 'a guard.sh refusal cannot be lifted'. (2) Because refusals are final, the block message MUST carry the way forward; guard.sh's g_alternative and scope-guard.sh's s_alternative are that, and a new rule without an entry there is a wall with no door beside it. deny[] holds the control set (this file, settings.local.json, guard.sh, scope-guard.sh, hooklib.sh, ratchet.config.sh), secrets/keys/.env, force push and branch deletion, the governing corpus, the domain's forbidden artifacts, and its banned-read files. Those are denied HERE and refused AGAIN in guard.sh/scope-guard.sh - two layers on purpose.",
  "_ratchet_note_decisions": "DECISIONS.md and .context/archive/decisions/** are deliberately NOT denied. The orchestrator must be able to append a decision entry, and CONTRACT 7.3 states that appending a decision must never be the failing action. Rollover (rewriting the hot file) is a different act and is refused by guard.sh under the rule id decisions-hot-rollover.",
